{"records":[{"id":"hzvpmrf5g3","title":"Quantitative phase imaging with temporal kinetics predicts hematopoietic stem cell diversity","description":"Innovative identification technologies for hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) have advanced the frontiers of stem cell biology. Clinically, the functional quality of HSCs profoundly influences the safety and therapeutic efficacy of stem cell treatments. However, most analytical techniques capture only a single snapshot, disregarding the temporal context. A comprehensive understanding of the temporal heterogeneity of HSCs necessitates live-cell, real-time and non-invasive analysis. Here, we developed a prediction system for HSC diversity by integrating single-HSC ex vivo expansion technology with quantitative phase imaging (QPI)-driven machine learning. By analyzing single-cell kinetics with QPI, we discovered previously undetectable diversity among HSCs that snapshot analysis fails to capture. Our QPI-driven algorithm quantitatively evaluates the stemness of individual HSCs and incorporates temporal information to significantly improve prediction accuracy. This platform marks a paradigm shift from \"identification\" to \"prediction\", enabling us to forecast HSC quality by analyzing their past temporal kinetics. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-05T07:22:24.586Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hzvpmrf5g3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hzvpmrf5g3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Takao","last_name":"Yogo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuichiro","last_name":"Iwamoto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hans Jiro","last_name":"Becker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Takaharu","last_name":"Kimura","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Reiko","last_name":"Ishida","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ayano","last_name":"Sugiyama-Finnis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tomomasa","last_name":"Yokomizo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Toshio","last_name":"Suda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sadao","last_name":"Ota","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Satoshi","last_name":"Yamazaki","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hematology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9sr7pb6c76","title":"Supplemental Tables - Accelerated osteocytic citrate production in chronic kidney disease is associated with protection of the kidney","description":"Supplemental Tables for manuscript: \"Accelerated osteocytic citrate production in chronic kidney disease is associated with protection of the kidney\". \n\nDatasets:\nTable S1: Differentially expressed genes in tibia cortices of control and AdKI mice (n=3 per group), analyzed via RNASeq and the Deseq2 algorithm.\nTable S2: Extracellular 13C-labeling of metabolites within spent media of osteocyte-enriched femora (Ocy-Fem) and osteocyte-enriched calvariae (Ocy-Cal) following 24h of ex vivo culture in [1,2-13C]-glucose (n=5 per group).\nTable S3: Intracellular 13C-labeling of metabolites extracted from osteocyte-enriched femora (Ocy-Fem) and osteocyte-enriched calvariae (Ocy-Cal) following 24h of ex vivo culture in [1,2-13C]-glucose (n=5 per group). Note: At the start (time 0h), it is assumed that M0 fragments predominate (100%) and no 13C-enrichment has taken place yet. Data at 9h and 24h are extracted from ex vivo cultured osteocyte-enriched bone organs.\nTable S4: Extracellular 13C-labeling of metabolites within spent media of osteocyte-enriched femora (Ocy-Fem) following 24h of ex vivo culture in [1,2-13C]-glucose or [U-13C]-glutamine (n=5 for control and AdKI WT mice; n=4 for control and AdKI Slc13a5R337*/R337* mice).\n\n(Accepted manuscript for publication in a peer-reviewed journal may differ)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-10T14:12:43.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9sr7pb6c76","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9sr7pb6c76"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jie Ren Gerald","last_name":"Har","orcid":"0000-0002-8071-469X"},{"first_name":"Lauren","last_name":"Surface","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Messenger RNA","Quadrupole Mass Spectrometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mrrxsgfrh2","title":"Results and raw data - Ex-vivo culture of the human heart","description":"Raw data for metabolomics and proteomics analysis performed on cultured living myocardial slices from HCM patients at different time points in culture (28 days).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-11T00:31:53.551Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mrrxsgfrh2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mrrxsgfrh2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ali","last_name":"Nassar","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Omics","Contractility","Tissue Culture"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztwnydbhsw","title":"G2 arrest primes hematopoietic stem cells for megakaryopoiesis","description":"In contrast to most hematopoietic lineages, megakaryocytes (MKs) can derive rapidly and directly from hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs). The underlying mechanism is unclear however. Here we show that DNA damage induces MK markers in HSCs and that G2 arrest, an integral part of the DNA damage response, suffices for MK priming followed by irreversible MK differentiation in HSCs, but not in progenitors. We also show that replication stress causes DNA damage in HSCs and is at least in part due to uracil misincorporation in vitro and in vivo.  Consistent with this notion, thymidine attenuated DNA damage, improved HSC maintenance and reduced the generation of CD41+ MK-committed HSCs. Replication stress and concomitant MK differentiation is therefore one of the barriers to HSC maintenance. DNA damage-induced MK priming may allow rapid generation of a lineage essential to immediate organismal survival, while also removing damaged cells from the HSC pool. \nThis dataset accompanies a manuscript in Cell Report, 2024, and contain the data from ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-05T06:57:20.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztwnydbhsw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztwnydbhsw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hans","last_name":"Snoeck","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Cycle","DNA Damage Response","Hematopoietic Stem Cell","General Hematopoiesis","Megakaryocytopoiesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x9xvy7y444","title":"Ex vivo WT zebrafish ERG data - Control and barium chloride treatment data for zebrafish bipolar cells","description":"Ex vivo WT zebrafish ERG data for the paper ‘Barium chloride preferentially alters ON-pathway signaling in the zebrafish retina’ (in preparation)\n\nControl (no drugs) data documents the naturally occurring response properties of the isolated zebrafish retina, recorded via ex vivo ERG. \nTreatment (BaCl₂ ) data documents the changes in responses following bath application of the potassium channel blocker barium chloride.\n\nInformation sheet documents all experiments conducted for this research. Files included are for successful experiments only. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-02T18:36:30.083Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x9xvy7y444","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x9xvy7y444"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aimee","last_name":"Saunders","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Electrophysiology","Retina","Visual Function","Electrophysiology of Retina","Retinal Neuron"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"scsgf27ywh","title":"Data for \"Calf fitness associates with early embryo and recipient metabolomes, and with calf epigenetic marks\"","description":"Metadata used, including metabolites identified in embryo culture medium and recipient plasma, along with calf parameters, as well as gene expression and methylation results in calf lymphocytes obtained after the first colostrum intake. This data were used in the study \"Calf fitness associates with early embryo and recipient metabolomes, and with calf epigenetic marks\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-02T00:09:57.626Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/scsgf27ywh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/scsgf27ywh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Isabel","last_name":"Gimeno","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Berdasco","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Miguel ","last_name":"Pato","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pascal","last_name":"Salvetti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Susana","last_name":"Carrocera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aurora","last_name":"García","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Enrique","last_name":"Gómez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Animal Health Management","Biomarker Research","Metabolomics","Gene Expression","DNA Methylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zc688j9g7s","title":"Metabolomics of human CD4+ T cells","description":"We performed a metabolomic experiment to study the steady-state levels of metabolites both ex-vivo and during CD4+ T cell activation. It is found that TCR stimulation led to a complex metabolic reprogramming within 72 hours.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-02T09:01:42.103Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zc688j9g7s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zc688j9g7s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"stefano","last_name":"biffo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tk3cn3rhb8","title":"Raw western blot data-Loss of p53 exacerbates autoimmunity by reprogramming propionyl-CoA metabolism and epigenetics in Treg cells","description":"Metabolic regulation is central to the tumor suppressor function of p53. Here we report that propionyl-CoA metabolic remodeling and epigenetic changes underpin p53-mediated restraint of autoimmunity through regulatory T (Treg) cells. By analyzing the human patients with autoimmune diseases, we found p53 expression was significantly reduced in Treg cells negatively correlating with abnormally elevated BCL-6 levels. p53 loss causes dysregulated immune homeostasis and dampens Tregs function in vitro and in vivo. Mechanistically, p53 transcriptionally activates ALDH6A1 expression and propionyl-CoA anabolism to upregulate functional Treg gene expression via histone propionylation. Treg-specific knockout of ALDH6A1 phenocopies the autoimmune responses of p53 deficiency, and propionyl-CoA restoration largely recovers Treg cell function in mice lacking p53 or ALDH6A1. Clinically, impaired p53-ALDH6A1-histone propionylation signaling is observed in AS and SLE patients and correlates with poor efficacy of first-line therapies in autoimmune patients. Together, these findings reveal a directly connection between propionyl-CoA metabolism and epigenetic changes, which is governed by p53 and is crucial for Treg cell function and immune tolerance suppression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-10T13:06:43.282Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tk3cn3rhb8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tk3cn3rhb8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"思懿","last_name":"谢","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s46dyhzmds","title":"Non-invasive bioimaging detection of methylmercury in the brain of living mice by protein templated gold nanoclusters ","description":"First,16-mer ferritin-like protein (7A) can transport through blood brain barrier as human H chain ferritin does, which was verified by in vivo living image. Second, 7A templated gold nanoclusters are prepared, which was identified by TEM. Third,the as-prepared 7A-Au NCs  are an effective probe for bioimaging MeHg+ detection in a brain-specific manner (living image in vivo and ex vivo) .","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-23T07:12:42.866Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s46dyhzmds","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s46dyhzmds"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Guanghua","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chenyan","last_name":"Lv","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuhua","last_name":"Yin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiuqing","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jinwen","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ferritin","Blood-Brain Barrier","Methylated Mercury"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cnv5p5rszz","title":"CHOP Deficiency Enhances Hematopoietic Stem Cell Function via Reducing ATF3/ROS Induced Cell Apoptosis","description":"Hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) reside in a quiescent niche to reserve their capacity of self-renewal. Upon hematopoietic injuries, HSCs enter the cell cycle and encounter protein homeostasis problems caused by accumulation of mis-folded proteins. However, the mechanism by which protein homeostasis influences HSC function and maintenance remains poorly understood. Here, we show that C/EBP homologous protein (CHOP), which is demonstrated previously, induces cell death upon tunicamycin induced unfolded protein response (UPR), plays an important role in HSCs regeneration. CHOP-/- mice showed normal hematopoietic stem and progenitor cell frequencies in steady state. However, when treated with 5-FU, CHOP deficiency resulted in higher survival rates, associated with an increased number of HSCs and reduced level of apoptosis. In serial competitive transplantation experiments, CHOP-/- HSCs showed a dramatic enhancement of repopulation ability and a reduction of protein aggresomes. Mechanistically, CHOP deletion causes reduced ATF3 expression and further leads to decreased protein aggregation and ROS. In addition, CHOP-/- HSCs exhibited an increased resistance to IR-induced DNA damage and improved HSCs homeostasis and function in telomere-dysfunctional (G3Terc-/-) mice. In summary, these findings disclose a new role of CHOP in the regulation of the HSCs function and homeostasis through reducing ATF3 and ROS signaling.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-08-27T04:57:54.562Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cnv5p5rszz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cnv5p5rszz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhencan","last_name":"shi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Blood Flow","Chip Formation"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":140117,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":184,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":140117,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139383,"restricted":69,"embargoed":665},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2349},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1415},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":694},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":483},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":393},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":336},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":277}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":137461},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1911},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":901},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":863},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":532},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":363}]}}